E-Commerce Analytics SQL Project 📊

Project Overview:
This project contains a suite of 10 Advanced SQL queries designed to analyze the full lifecycle of an e-commerce business. The scripts cover critical areas including Financial Health, User Behavior, and Marketing Performance.

The goal of this project is to demonstrate how raw transaction and event logs can be transformed into actionable business insights using industry-standard metrics.

📂 Files- Dataset Link- https://www.kaggle.com/datasets/geethasagarbonthu/marketing-and-e-commerce-analytics-dataset
`ecommerce.sql_analysis`: The master script containing all 10 annotated queries.



🧠 Business Insights Generated
This project answers high-impact business questions across three key domains:

1. Financial Performance 💰
AOV Trends: Identifying whether customer basket size is growing or shrinking month-over-month.
Refund Analysis: Pinpointing specific product categories that are driving revenue loss due to quality issues.
Discount Strategy: Evaluating if deep discounts are effectively targeting high-tier loyal customers or eroding margins.

2. User Behavior & Product Health 🛒
Cart Abandonment: Diagnosing friction points in the checkout funnel by calculating the exact drop-off rate between "Cart Add" and "Purchase".
Customer Retention: Using CTEs to calculate the Repeat Purchase Rate, separating one-time buyers from loyal returning customers.
Churn Risk (RFM): Calculating Recency to identify "cold" customers in specific loyalty tiers who need re-engagement.

3. Marketing & A/B Testing 📈
CLV by Channel: determining which acquisition sources (e.g., Paid Search vs. Organic) drive the highest **Customer Lifetime Value**.
A/B Test Analysis: Measuring the statistical uplift of experimental features by comparing Conversion Rates between Control and Experiment groups.
Campaign ROI: Identifying which marketing objectives are attracting the highest-value users (Revenue per Acquired Customer).

🛠️ Technical Skills Demonstrated
Complex Aggregations: `GROUP BY`, `HAVING`, and multi-level data summarization.
Advanced Joins: `LEFT JOIN` vs `INNER JOIN` logic to handle missing data (e.g., customers who haven't bought yet).
Window Functions & CTEs: Using `WITH` clauses for multi-step logic (e.g., calculating order counts before grouping).
Data Cleaning: Handling `NULL` values with `COALESCE` and preventing division errors with `SAFE_DIVIDE`.
String Manipulation: Using `LIKE` patterns and `LOWER()` for robust case-insensitive matching.
Date Functions: `DATE_DIFF`, `FORMAT_DATE`, and timestamp extraction for cohort analysis.

